{
  "source_poem_zh": "纤云弄巧，\n飞星传恨，\n银汉迢迢暗度。\n金风玉露一相逢，\n便胜却人间无数。\n柔情似水，\n佳期如梦，\n忍顾鹊桥归路。\n两情若是久长时，\n又岂在朝朝暮暮。",
  "critical_units": [
    {
      "id": "song_07_u1",
      "source_span": "纤云弄巧",
      "meaning": "云被写成有意变换巧妙形态。"
    },
    {
      "id": "song_07_u2",
      "source_span": "飞星传恨",
      "meaning": "星光被写成传递离愁的媒介。"
    },
    {
      "id": "song_07_u3",
      "source_span": "金风玉露一相逢",
      "meaning": "借秋日风露写一次珍贵相会，带有七夕文化意象。"
    },
    {
      "id": "song_07_u4",
      "source_span": "又岂在朝朝暮暮",
      "meaning": "由相聚景象转为判断情意不必以日日相守为条件。"
    }
  ],
  "translations_to_score": [
    {
      "opaque_id": "x7d470ae5e90c75",
      "translation": "Wisps of cloud work their magic;\\nshooting stars convey their longing.\\nAcross the vast, dark Silver River, they steal away.\\nWhen golden autumn wind and jade-white dew bring them together,\\none meeting outshines countless earthly ones.\\n\\nTheir tender love flows like water;\\ntheir long-awaited meeting, like a dream.\\nHow can they bear to look back at the magpie bridge,\\nthe road home?\\nIf two hearts remain true for the long, long years,\\nwhy need they be together night after night, day after day?",
      "notes": [
        "The poem refers to the lovers of Chinese legend, separated by the Silver River (the Milky Way) and reunited once a year across a bridge of magpies."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xaeae67fb641dcc",
      "translation": "Fine clouds weave their delicate patterns,\nFlying stars convey their longing;\nAcross the distant Milky Way, they meet in secret.\nOne meeting in the autumn wind and jade-bright dew\nOutshines countless unions in the mortal world.\nTenderness flows like water,\nThe cherished tryst is like a dream;\nI cannot bear to look back at the Magpie Bridge, the road home.\nIf two hearts can remain in love forever,\nMust they be bound to morning and evening together?",
      "notes": [
        "The Magpie Bridge evokes the traditional Qixi tale of lovers granted a brief annual meeting across the Milky Way."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "x879fd69480048d",
      "translation": "Slender clouds weave their clever patterns;\nshooting stars carry a message of sorrow.\nAcross the vast Silver River, they meet in secret.\nOne meeting in autumn’s golden wind and jade dew\noutshines countless encounters in the mortal world.\nTender feelings flow like water;\nthe promised hour is a dream.\nHow can they bear to look toward the magpie bridge,\nthe road that leads them back apart?\nIf love between two hearts endures,\nwhy must they be together night after night, day after day?",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "x6823004dbd2f60",
      "translation": "Delicate clouds weave their cunning art;\nFalling stars convey a heart's regret.\nAcross the long, dim River of Heaven,\nthey steal toward their meeting.\nOne meeting in autumn's golden wind and jade-white dew\noutshines a thousand worldly embraces.\nTenderness flows like water;\nthe cherished hour, a dream.\nHow can they bear to look back\nalong the Magpie Bridge, toward home?\nIf two hearts endure through time,\nmust love be measured by\nmorning and evening, side by side?",
      "notes": [
        "The River of Heaven refers to the Milky Way; the Magpie Bridge evokes the traditional Qixi imagery.",
        "The translation preserves the poem's contrast between brief reunion and enduring love."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "x35ad3811d5914f",
      "translation": "Fine clouds weave their art,\nFlying stars bear sorrow;\nIn secret they cross the distant Silver River.\nOne meeting in golden wind and jade-bright dew\nSurpasses countless joys on earth.\nTenderness flows like water,\nTheir time together seems a dream—\nHow bear to look back at the homeward magpie bridge?\nIf love between two hearts endures,\nWhy must they be together every dawn and dusk?",
      "notes": [
        "The Silver River is the Milky Way. The magpie bridge allows the separated lovers, the Cowherd and Weaver Girl, to meet."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xcbfbfbfe196b0c",
      "translation": "Slender clouds weave their art;\nshooting stars carry longing\nacross the distant, darkened River of Stars.\nOne meeting in autumn’s golden wind and jade dew\noutweighs countless earthly unions.\nTenderness flows like water;\nthe promised hour is a dream.\nHow can we bear to look back\nat the magpie bridge and the road home?\nIf two hearts can love for the long years,\nwhat need is there to be together\nmorning after morning, evening after evening?",
      "notes": [
        "The magpie bridge evokes the traditional Qixi festival imagery of a lovers’ meeting."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "source_poem_zh": "莫听穿林打叶声，何妨吟啸且徐行。\n竹杖芒鞋轻胜马，谁怕？一蓑烟雨任平生。",
  "critical_units": [
    {
      "id": "song_08_u1",
      "source_span": "莫听穿林打叶声",
      "meaning": "劝说不必被雨声牵制，劝说对象未明确。"
    },
    {
      "id": "song_08_u2",
      "source_span": "何妨吟啸且徐行",
      "meaning": "提出从容行走的行动选择。"
    },
    {
      "id": "song_08_u3",
      "source_span": "竹杖芒鞋轻胜马",
      "meaning": "把徒步装备写得胜过骑马，轻重可含心境意味。"
    },
    {
      "id": "song_08_u4",
      "source_span": "一蓑烟雨任平生",
      "meaning": "以蓑衣烟雨概括从容面对风雨，可兼指现实天气与人生处境。"
    }
  ],
  "translations_to_score": [
    {
      "opaque_id": "xa22f9bbf20d1d5",
      "translation": "Heed not the rain piercing the woods and beating the leaves;\nWhy not chant and whistle, and walk at ease?\nBamboo staff and straw sandals—lighter than riding a horse.\nWho fears?\nA straw cape, mist and rain—let them be mine for all my days.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xa4ba8aadaeb50e",
      "translation": "Heed not the rain that lashes leaves and threads the woods;\nWhy not chant and whistle, walking at ease?\nBamboo staff and straw sandals—lighter than riding a horse.\nWho’s afraid?\nA straw cloak, a lifetime of mist and rain—let them come.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xcf2af4afe7ca09",
      "translation": "Pay no heed to rain drumming through the trees;\nwhy not sing, and stroll at an easy pace?\nBamboo staff and straw sandals outshine a horse—\nwho’s afraid? Let mist and rain enfold my life.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "x21d5ff33350071",
      "translation": "Listen not to the rain beating through the trees,\nwhy not sing aloud and stroll at ease?\nBamboo staff, straw sandals—lighter than a horse.\nWho’s afraid? In a rain cape through mist and rain,\nI’ll spend my life this way.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xb966e6123756c3",
      "translation": "Pay no heed to rain piercing the woods and beating the leaves;\nWhy not chant and whistle, and walk at ease?\nWith bamboo staff and straw sandals, I travel lighter than on horseback.\nWho’s afraid?\nIn a straw rain cape, let me weather mist and rain all my life.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xe0acf0d0194a01",
      "translation": "Hear how rain through the woods beats on the leaves—\nwhy not sing, and stroll at an unhurried pace?\nA bamboo staff, straw sandals: lighter than a horse.\nWho’s afraid? In a cape of mist and rain,\nI’ll let life’s storms pass as they will.",
      "notes": []
    }
  ]
}

