{
  "source_poem_zh": "晨起动征铎，\n客行悲故乡。\n鸡声茅店月，\n人迹板桥霜。\n槲叶落山路，\n枳花明驿墙。\n因思杜陵梦，\n凫雁满回塘。",
  "critical_units": [
    {
      "id": "tang_07_u1",
      "source_span": "客行悲故乡",
      "meaning": "行旅途中直接点出对故乡的悲思。"
    },
    {
      "id": "tang_07_u2",
      "source_span": "鸡声茅店月",
      "meaning": "以鸡鸣、客店和月色并置早行时所闻所见。"
    },
    {
      "id": "tang_07_u3",
      "source_span": "人迹板桥霜",
      "meaning": "以桥上人迹和霜痕提示清早已有行人经过。"
    },
    {
      "id": "tang_07_u4",
      "source_span": "因思杜陵梦",
      "meaning": "从当前旅途转入关于杜陵的梦或回想。"
    }
  ],
  "translations_to_score": [
    {
      "opaque_id": "xdb4f779329f3c2",
      "translation": "At dawn the travel bells begin to stir;\nA traveler grieves for home.\nCockcrow; a thatched inn beneath the moon.\nFootprints on the plank bridge, traced in frost.\nOak leaves fall along the mountain road;\nBitter-orange blossoms brighten the post-station wall.\nAnd so I think of my dream of Duling:\nDucks and geese fill the pond I long to return to.",
      "notes": [
        "Duling was the poet’s home district near Chang’an."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xc68e515be3c1de",
      "translation": "At dawn, the travel bells stir;\nfar from home, the traveler grieves.\nRooster cries; a thatched inn; the moon.\nFootprints on the frost-rimed plank bridge.\nOak leaves fall along the mountain road;\nwild orange blossoms brighten the post-house wall.\nThen comes the dream of Duling:\nducks and geese fill the winding pond.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "x49957d672711d7",
      "translation": "At dawn I rise; the journey bells ring out.\nFar from home, I travel, sick with longing.\nCockcrow, moonlight on the thatched inn;\nFootprints in frost on the bridge of planks.\nOak leaves fall along the mountain road;\nBitter-orange blossoms brighten the post-house wall.\nI think then of my dream of Duling:\nWild ducks and geese fill the curving pond.",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "x9be6c14dddcfdd",
      "translation": "At dawn, the journey bells stir;\nA traveler grieves for his native land.\nCockcrow, the thatched inn beneath the moon;\nFootprints mark the frosted plank bridge.\nOak leaves fall along the mountain road;\nPoncirus flowers brighten the posthouse wall.\nThen I dream of Duling once more—\nDucks and geese fill the returning pond.",
      "notes": [
        "“Duling” is a place associated with Chang’an and carries the sense of remembered home."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xa535e56196e6f7",
      "translation": "At dawn I rise; the journey bells ring out.\nFar from home, the traveler grieves.\nCockcrow, a thatched inn, the moon;\nFootprints, a plank bridge, frost.\nOak leaves fall along the mountain road;\nBitter-orange blossoms brighten the post-house wall.\nAnd so I think of dreams of Duling:\nDucks and geese fill the curving pond.",
      "notes": [
        "Duling, near Chang’an, evokes the poet’s home.",
        "The third and fourth lines preserve the original’s spare juxtaposition of images."
      ]
    },
    {
      "opaque_id": "xc16d7b6e676fe7",
      "translation": "At dawn, the travel bells stir;  \nA traveler, sorrowing for his home.  \nCockcrow, a thatched inn, and moonlight;  \nFootprints on the plank bridge, frost-white.  \nOak leaves fall along the mountain road;  \nTrifoliate orange blossoms brighten the post-station wall.  \nThinking of dreams of Duling,  \nWild ducks and geese fill the returning pond.",
      "notes": []
    }
  ]
}

